<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<handout>
  <title>Section 2.3: The Sine and Cosine Functions</title>
  <p>
    <alert>MTH 124</alert>
  </p>

  
<sidebyside widths="20% 60%" >
<image  >
      <shortdescription>
        A unit circle showing the sine function.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-3-sine">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="20">
          <definition>
            f(t)=(cos(t*pi/12),sin(t*pi/12))
          </definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-1.2,-1.2,1.2,1.2)">
            <axes decorations="no"/>
            <circle center="(0,0)" radius="1" stroke="blue"/>
            <line endpoints="((0,0),f(3.6))" stroke="red"/>
            <label anchor="1/2*f(3.6)" alignment="nw" ><m>1</m></label>
            <angle-marker points="(f(0),(0,0),f(3.6))" offset="(0,-5)">
              <m>t</m>
            </angle-marker>  
            <point p="f(3.6)" alignment="e"><m>(a,b)</m></point>     
            <label anchor="(.8,1.2)"><m>\sin(t)=b</m></label>   
            <label anchor="(.8,1.05)"><m>\cos(t)=a</m></label> 


          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
  <p>
    Have your unit circles out!  Use them as a reference.  We are going to turn each coordinate 
    of the points on the unit circle into a new function.
  </p>
     </sidebyside>

  <definition>
    <title>Definition 2.3.4</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Given a central angle in the unit circle with angle <m>t</m>
 radians and the terminal point <m>(a,b)</m>,
 as shown in the graph, we define the <term>sine of <m>t</m></term>, denoted <m>\sin(t)</m>,
 by the rule <md>\sin(t) = b.</md>
 That is, the <m>y</m>-coordinate on the unit circle IS the <m>\sin(t)</m>!</p>
    </statement>
  </definition>



  <definition>
    <title>Definition 2.3.8.</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Given a central angle in the unit circle that measures <m>t</m>
 radians and the terminal point <m>(a,b)</m>,
 as shown in the graph, we define the <term>cosine of <m>t</m></term>, denoted <m>\cos(t)</m>,
 by the rule <md>\cos(t) = a.</md>
 That is, the <m>x</m>-coordinate on the unit circle IS the <m>\cos(t)</m>!</p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

    


  <p>We'll start with the sine function.  A table of values and a graph of the function <m>\sin(t)</m> are below.</p>
  
<tabular>
