  <activity>
    <title>Activity 2.3.2. The cosine function</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>Let <m>k=g(t)</m>
 be the function that tracks the <m>x</m>-coordinate of a point traversing the unit circle counterclockwise from <m>(1,0)</m>.
 That is, <m>g(t) = \cos(t)</m>.
 Use the information we know about the unit circle to respond to the following questions.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the exact value of 
 <m>\cos(\frac{\pi}{6})</m>?
 of <m>\cos(\frac{5\pi}{6})</m>
? <m>\cos(-\frac{\pi}{3})</m>?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-3-soln">
        <p>
          See the x-coordinate on the unit circle that corresponds to those angles. 

        </p>
        <p>
          <m>\cos(\frac{\pi}{6}) = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m>. <m>\cos(\frac{5\pi}{6}) = -\frac{\sqrt3}{2}</m>. <m>\cos(-\frac{\pi}{3}) = \frac12</m> (same angle as <m>\frac{5\pi}{3}</m> or think of it as going 
          "backwards" or clockwise by an angle of <m>\frac{\pi}{3}</m>.)
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task >
      <statement>
        <p>Complete the following table with the exact values of <m>k=g(t) = \cos(t)</m>
 that correspond to the stated inputs <m>t</m>.</p>
        <tabular>
          <row bottom="minor">
    <cell>t</cell>
    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\pi}{6}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\pi}{4}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\pi}{3}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{2\pi}{3}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{3\pi}{4}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{5\pi}{6}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\pi</m></cell>
   
  </row>
  <row  bottom="major">
    <cell><m>\cos(t)</m></cell>
    <cell><m>1</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt3}2</m></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    
  </row>
  
   <row bottom="minor">
    <cell>t</cell>
    
    <cell><m>\pi</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{7\pi}{6}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{5\pi}{4}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{4\pi}{3}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{3\pi}{2}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{5\pi}{3}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{7\pi}{4}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{11\pi}{6}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>2\pi</m></cell>
  </row>


  <row>
    <cell><m>\cos(t)</m></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
  </row>
</tabular>
        
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-3-soln">
        <p>
          See your unit circle and pull out the x-coordinates for each angle.
        </p>
          <tabular>
          <row bottom="minor">
    <cell>t</cell>
    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\pi}{6}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\pi}{4}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\pi}{3}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{2\pi}{3}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{3\pi}{4}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{5\pi}{6}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\pi</m></cell>
   
  </row>
  <row  bottom="major">
    <cell><m>\cos(t)</m></cell>
    <cell><m>1</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt3}2</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt2}2</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac12</m></cell>
    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
    <cell><m>-\frac12</m></cell>
    <cell><m>-\frac{\sqrt2}2</m></cell>
    <cell><m>-\frac{\sqrt3}2</m></cell>
    <cell>-1</cell>
    
  </row>
  
   <row bottom="minor">
    <cell>t</cell>
    
    <cell><m>\pi</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{7\pi}{6}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{5\pi}{4}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{4\pi}{3}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{3\pi}{2}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{5\pi}{3}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{7\pi}{4}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{11\pi}{6}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>2\pi</m></cell>
  </row>


  <row>
    <cell><m>\cos(t)</m></cell>
    <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
    <cell><m>-\frac{\sqrt3}2</m></cell>
    <cell><m>-\frac{\sqrt2}2</m></cell>
    <cell><m>-\frac{1}2</m></cell>
    <cell>0</cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{1}2</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt2}2</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt3}2</m></cell>
    <cell>1</cell>
  </row>
</tabular>
        
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task >
      <statement>
        <p>On the axes provided below, sketch an accurate graph of <m>{k=g(t) = \cos(t)}</m>.
 Label the exact location of several key points on the curve.</p>
         <image width="85%">
      <shortdescription>
        A graph of the cosine function for you to fill in.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-3-cosine">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,200)" margins="5">
          <definition>
            f(t)=cos(t)
          </definition>
          <definition>points=['0','pi/6']</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-pi/4,-1.25,2*pi+pi/4,1.25)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes" h-pi-format="yes" 
            spacings="((-pi/4,pi/4,9*pi/4), (-1.5,.25,1.5))" 
            hlabels = "(0,pi/4,2*pi)" xlabel="t"/>
            
            <label anchor="(2.9,1.1)"><m>g(t) = \cos(t)</m></label>  
        
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-3-soln">
        
        <image width="85%">
      <shortdescription>
        A graph of the cosine function.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-3-cosine-soln">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,200)" margins="5">
          <definition>
            f(t)=cos(t)
          </definition>
          <definition>points=['0','pi/6']</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-pi/4,-1.25,2*pi+pi/4,1.25)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes" h-pi-format="yes" 
            spacings="((-pi/4,pi/4,9*pi/4), (-1.5,.25,1.5))" 
            hlabels = "(0,pi/4,2*pi)" xlabel="t"/>
            <graph function="f"/>
            <label anchor="(2.9,1.1)"><m>g(t) = \cos(t)</m></label> 
            <point p="(0,1)"><m>(0,1)</m> </point> 
            <point p="(pi/6,f(pi/6))"><m>(\frac\pi6,\frac{\sqrt3}2)</m> </point> 
            <point p="(pi/4,f(pi/4))" alignment="sw"><m>(\frac\pi4,\frac{\sqrt2}2)</m> </point> 
            <point p="(pi/3,f(pi/3))" alignment="e"><m>(\frac\pi3,\frac12)</m> </point> 
        
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
        
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the exact value of 
 <m>\cos(\frac{11\pi}{4})</m>?
 of <m>\cos(\frac{14\pi}{3})</m>
?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-3-soln">
        <p>
          *Going around the circle by <m>2\pi</m> doesn't change the angle, it just changes the number of times around the circle. 
          So an angle of <m>\frac{11\pi}{4}</m> is equivalent to an angle of 
          <md>\frac{11\pi}{4} - 2\pi = \frac{11\pi}{4}-\frac{2\pi*4}{4} = \frac{11\pi}{4}-\frac{8\pi}{4} = \frac{3\pi}{4}.</md>
          So <m>\cos(\frac{11\pi}{4}) = \cos(\frac{11\pi}{4}-2\pi) = \cos(\frac{3\pi}{4})=-\frac{\sqrt2}{2}</m>. 
          Similarly, <m>\frac{14\pi}{3}</m> is more than two full laps around the circle, so that angle is 
          equivalent to the angle 
          <md>
            \frac{14\pi}{3} - 2*(2\pi) = \frac{14\pi}{3} - \frac{4\pi * 3}{1*3} = \frac{14\pi}{3}-\frac{12\pi}{3} = \frac{2\pi}{3}
          </md>
          so <m>\cos(\frac{14\pi}{3}) = \cos(\frac{14\pi}{3}-2*(2\pi)) = \cos(\frac{2\pi}{3}) = -\frac12</m>.
          
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.6in">
      <statement>
        <p>Give four different values of <m>t</m>
 for which <m>\cos(t) = -\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-3-soln">
        <p>
          The easiest ones to see are <m>t = \frac{5\pi}{6}, \frac{7\pi}{6}</m>, but we can add or subtract any
          multiple of <m>2\pi</m> to those and be at the same point on the unit circle, so 
          <md>t = \ldots, \frac{5\pi}{6}, \frac{7\pi}{6}, \frac{17\pi}{6}, \frac{19\pi}{6}, \frac{29\pi}{6}, \frac{31\pi}{6}, \ldots</md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.6in">
      <statement>
        <p>How is the graph of <m>\cos(t)</m>
 different from the graph of <m>\sin(t)</m>?
 How are the graphs similar?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-3-soln">
        <p>
          Lots of things you could say.  The biggest difference (according to me) is the 
          starting point - cosine starts at 1 not 0, that is <m>\cos(0) = 1</m> whereas 
          <m>\sin(0)=0</m>, so cosine starts at the top of its range and goes down first whereas sine starts in the middle and goes up first.
        </p>
        <p>
          They are exactly the same shape, just shifted by <m>\frac\pi2</m> to the 
          right/left!
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <p>Recall that the sine and cosine functions are just tracking the <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>-coordinates as we move around the unit circle. See <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/0xe">gvsu.edu/s/0xe</url>
and click on sine or cosine for an animation.</p>

  <assemblage>
    <title>Properties of the sine and cosine functions</title>
    <p>For both <m>f(t) = \sin(t)</m> and <m>g(t) =\cos(t)</m>:</p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>the domain is all real numbers</p></li>
        <li><p>the range is <m>[-1,1]</m></p></li>
        <li><p>the midline is <m>y=0</m></p></li>
        <li><p>the amplitude is <m>a=1</m></p></li>
        <li><p>the period is <m>p=2\pi</m></p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <assemblage >
    <title>The Fundamental Trigonometric Identity</title>
    <p>For any real number <m>t</m>, <md>\sin^2(t) + \cos^2(t) = 1.</md></p>
  </assemblage>

  <p workspace="1in">
     
  </p>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 2.3.3</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>Use the graph below that shows both <m>\sin(t)</m> and <m>\cos(t)</m> on the same plot to help answer the following questions.</p>
       <image width = "85%">
      <shortdescription>
        A graph of the sine and cosine functions.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-3-both">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,200)" margins="5">
          <definition>
            f(t)=sin(t)
          </definition>
          <definition>
            g(t)=cos(t)
            </definition>
          <definition>points=['0','pi/6']</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-pi/4,-1.25,2*pi+pi/4,1.25)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes" h-pi-format="yes" 
            spacings="((-pi/4,pi/4,9*pi/4), (-1.5,.25,1.5))" 
            hlabels = "(0,pi/4,2*pi)" xlabel="t"/>
            <graph function="f"/>
            <label anchor="(2.9,1.1)"><m>f(t) = \sin(t)</m></label>  
            <graph function="g" dash="4 4" stroke="red"/>
            <label anchor="(1.9,-1.1)"><m>g(t) = \cos(t)</m></label> 
            <vector tail="(1.3,g(1.3))" v="(pi/2,0)"/>
        
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Give an example of the largest interval you can find on which <m>\sin(t)</m>
 is decreasing.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-3-soln">
        <p>
          The most obvious from this graph is <m>\left(\frac\pi2, \frac{3\pi}2\right)</m>, but there are infinitely many if we 
          shift (right/left) by a multiple of <m>2\pi</m>.  (Same as <m>\frac\pi2 \lt t \lt \frac{3\pi}2</m>)
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Give an example of the largest interval you can find on which <m>\sin(t)</m>
 is decreasing and concave down.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-3-soln">
        <p>
          The <m>\sin(t)</m> is concave down (pessimistic) whenever it is above the midline <m>y=0</m>. 
          It is also decreasing in that second half of that interval.  So one example interval is 
          <m>\left(\frac\pi2, \pi\right)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Give an example of the largest interval you can find on which <m>\cos(t)</m>
 is increasing.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-3-soln">
        <p>
          The most obvious from the graph is <m>(\pi,2\pi)</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Give an example of the largest interval you can find on which <m>\cos(t)</m>
 is increasing and concave up.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-3-soln">
        <p>
          It is concave up when it is below the midline, and it is also increasing on the second half 
          of that interval, so one example is <m>(\pi,\frac{3\pi}{2})</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Without doing any computation, on which interval is the average rate of change of <m>g(t)=\cos(t)</m>
 greater: <m>[\pi,\pi+0.01]</m>
 or <m>[\frac{3\pi}{2},\frac{3\pi}{2}+0.01]</m>?
 Why?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-3-soln">
        <p>
          The slope around <m>[\pi,\pi+0.01]</m> is about 0 (it's flat at the top), 
          but between <m>[\frac{3\pi}{2},\frac{3\pi}{2}+0.01]</m> it has the steepest slope.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>In general, how would you characterize the locations on the sine and cosine graphs where the functions are increasing or decreasingly most rapidly?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-3-soln">
        <p>
          Sine is increasing/decreasing most rapidly around <m>t\approx 0, \pi, 2\pi, \ldots</m>
          and cosine around <m>t\approx \frac\pi2, \frac{3\pi}{2}, \frac{5\pi}2, \ldots</m>
          that is near the midline for both!
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Thinking from the perspective of the unit circle, for which quadrants of the <m>x</m>-<m>y</m>
 plane is <m>\cos(t)</m>
 negative for an angle <m>t</m> 
 that lies in that quadrant?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-3-soln">
        <p>
          Cosine is the x-coordinate, which is negative in quadrants II and III.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 2.2.4 Going between</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>Note that almost all computing devices can evaluate <m>\sin(t)</m> and <m>\cos(t)</m> for different values of <m>t</m>, but we need to be <term>careful to tell it whether we are in degrees or radians!</term>  In desmos, the option to choose is within the wrench at the top right of the page.  Being aware of that, find the exact values whenever possible, otherwise round to at least 4 decimal places below.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the <m>x</m>-coordinate of the point on the unit circle that lies in the third quadrant and whose 
<m>y</m>-coordinate is 
<m>y=-\frac34</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-3-soln">
        <p>
          With <m>y=-\frac34</m>, the Fundamental Trig Identity says 
          <m>{\ds x^2 + (-\frac34)^2 = 1}</m>, that is <m>{\ds x^2 + \frac9{16} = 1}</m>.  Rearranging:
          <m>{x^2 = 1-\frac9{16} = \frac{16}{16}-\frac{9}{16} = \frac{7}{16}}</m>.  Thus 
          <md>
            x = \pm \frac{\sqrt{7}}{4}
          </md>
          but we are in quadrant III and so the x-coordinate must be negative.  Thus <m>{x = -\frac{\sqrt7}{4}}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the <m>y</m>-coordinate of the point on the unit circle generated by a central angle opening counterclockwise with one side on the positive 
<m>x</m>-axis that measures 
<m>t=2</m> radians.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-3-soln">
        <p>
          Since <m>\frac\pi2\approx 1.57</m> and <m>\pi \approx 3.14</m> then 
          <m>2</m> radians is in between <m>\frac\pi2</m> and <m>\pi</m>, that is we are in 
          Quadrant II, where the y-coordinate is positive.  So <m>y=\sin(2)</m> is the exact answer, and it turns into 
          approximately <m>0.9093</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the 
<m>x</m>-coordinate of the point on the unit circle generated by a central angle with one side on the positive 
<m>x</m>-axis that measures 
<m>t=-3.05</m> radians. (With the negative radian measure, we view the angle as opening clockwise from its initial side on the positive 
<m>x</m>-axis.)</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-3-soln">
        <p>
          We end up in Quadrant III (we go "backwards" or clockwise by <m>3.05</m> radians which is just short of <m>\pi</m> radians). 
          <m>x = \cos(-3.05) \approx -0.996</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the value of <m>\cos(t)</m>
 where <m>t</m>
 is an angle in Quadrant II that satisfies 
 <m>\sin(t)=\frac12</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-3-soln">
        <p>
          We are looking for the point in Quadrant II that corresponds to <m>\sin(t)=\frac12</m>, or 
          the y-coordinate is <m>\frac12</m>.  This happens at <m>t=\frac{5\pi}{6}</m> (which I don't need to know, because 
          the x-y coordinates come in pairs I know...) so the x-coordinate is <m>-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}</m> or in other words 
          <m>{\cos(t) = -\frac{\sqrt3}{2}}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the value of <m>\sin(t)</m>
 where <m>t</m>
 is an angle in Quadrant III for which <m>\cos(t) = -0.7</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-3-soln">
        <p>
          In Quadrant III, both <m>\sin(t)</m> and <m>\cos(t)</m> are negative. 
          Fundamental Trig Identity: <m>\sin^2(t)+\cos^2(t) = 1</m> or since 
          <m>\cos(t)=-0.7</m> then 
          <md>
            \sin^2(t)+(-0.7)^2 = 1
          </md>
          or <m>\sin^2(t) = 0.51</m> and since <m>{\sin^2(t) = (\sin(t))^2}</m>, then
          <m>{(\sin(t))^2 = 0.51}</m> or <m>\sin(t) =\pm 0.714</m>.  Since we are in 
          Quadrant III, then <m>\sin(t) = -0.714</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the average rate of change of <m>f(t) = \sin(t)</m>
 on the intervals <m>[0.1,0.2]</m>
 and <m>[0.8,0.9]</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-3-soln">
        <p>
          <md>AV_{[0.1,0.2]} = \frac{\sin(0.2) - \sin(0.1)}{0.2-0.1}\approx 0.988</md>
          <md>AV_{[0.8,0.9]} = \frac{\sin(0.9) - \sin(0.8)}{0.9-0.8}\approx 0.6597</md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the average rate of change of <m>g(t) = \cos(t)</m> 
 on the intervals <m>[0.1,0.2]</m>
 and <m>[0.8,0.9]</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-3-soln">
        <p>
          <md>AV_{[0.1,0.2]} = \frac{\cos(0.2) - \cos(0.1)}{0.2-0.1}\approx -0.149</md>
          <md>AV_{[0.8,0.9]} = \frac{\cos(0.9) - \cos(0.8)}{0.9-0.8}\approx -0.751</md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </activity>

</handout>
